#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ai-os-tickets — the ticket records are the state; every view of them is derived.

  ai-os tickets list [--project P] [--state S] [--json]   the active set, from the records
  ai-os tickets next [--project P] [--goal G] [--limit N] [--why] [--json]
                                                           deterministic recommendation:
                                                           Do now / Owner decisions /
                                                           Blocked / Paused / Quick wins /
                                                           Future
  ai-os tickets new --title T --project P [metadata flags] [--next-action "…"] [--dry-run]
                                                           create a ticket — refused
                                                           outright if intent metadata is
                                                           incomplete (see below)
  ai-os tickets promote <ID> --relation {required|optional|blocks_parent|parent}
                             [metadata flags] [--dry-run]
                                                           move a future_candidate ticket
                                                           into scheduled work, supplying
                                                           only what is given explicitly
  ai-os tickets index [--project P] [--write]             regenerate a project's board table
  ai-os tickets doctor [--project P]                      structural checks over the records
  ai-os tickets log <ID> "<text>"                         one dated entry, nothing else
  ai-os tickets checkpoint <ID> --note "…" [--next "…"]   durable state at a boundary
  ai-os tickets archive [--project P] [--apply]           move done/cancelled tickets whole
                                                           into tickets/archive/{AIOS,Atlas}/
  ai-os tickets archive --auto [--project P] [--dry-run]  reconciliation: completion-gated,
                                                           idempotent (T-044)
  ai-os tickets archive --list [--project P]              read-only: list archived tickets
  ai-os tickets show <ID>                                 read-only: full record, live or
                                                           archived, clearly labeled
  ai-os tickets search <query>                            read-only: substring search over
                                                           id/title/goal/body, both sets

## Smart Automatic Ticket Archive System (T-044)

`checkpoint --state done` or `--state cancelled` runs the completion gate immediately after
writing the new state, and archives the ticket right then if it passes — no separate
command needed for the common case. `archive --auto` is the reconciliation path for
anything marked done/cancelled by an older tool or a manual edit: same gate, same result,
safe to re-run. Neither ever touches a ticket that fails the gate; the exact reason is
printed and the record is left byte-for-byte alone. `show`/`search`/`archive --list` never
trigger archiving — read-only commands never mutate anything. Priority never decides
eligibility, only where a result lands in the generated archive index (Completed/Cancelled
x Level 1-5/Unspecified, plus a separate Legacy AIOS section that predates the priority
system). There is no `unarchive`/restore command (deferred owner decision, same as T-024's).

## Smart Dynamic Ticket System (T-041)

`next` never invents a priority, a goal match, or a relationship — every score component
and every line of `--why` reads a field the ticket itself declared (`priority`, `relation`/
`parent`, `goal`, `unblocks`, `due`, `blocked_by`, `effort`/`risk`). A missing field is
reported (lower `confidence`, a named gap in `reasons`) and ranked cautiously; it is never
guessed to make the output look complete. `aios_tickets.classify_and_rank` is the one
implementation of the bucket and scoring rules — this command only selects, limits and
renders what that function returns, and `ai-os context`'s recommended-next-actions view
calls the same function, so the two can never disagree about what is recommended.

## Ticket lifecycle: `new` and `promote` (T-043)

The entry gate for the system above: `new` refuses to write a ticket (nothing touched)
whose intent metadata is incomplete (`priority`/`goal`/`requirement` — the same three
fields `next`'s `confidence` already downgrades for), and `promote` refuses to move a
`future_candidate` ticket into scheduled work under the same rule. Neither command ever
invents a value — every field either comes from an explicit flag or is left absent and
reported as an error; a ticket that would need a guessed value to pass simply is not
created or promoted. Both call `aios_tickets.lifecycle_issues`, the one shared rule set
`doctor` already checks after the fact — so nothing accepted here can later surprise
`doctor`, and nothing `doctor` flags today could have been created through this gate. A
`future_candidate` ticket is the one deliberate exemption from the three required fields
(a parked idea worth tracking as a ticket, not yet a commitment) — `promote` is the only
path that changes that relation, and it always re-imposes the full requirement.

## Archiving (T-024)

A ticket's `state` never changes because its directory moved — archiving is a physical
relocation of a `done`/`cancelled` record from `tickets/<ID>/` to
`tickets/archive/{AIOS,Atlas}/<ID>/`, nothing else. The id, its frontmatter, its history and
every reference to it by id keep working: `ai-os tickets show/log/checkpoint <ID>`,
`ai-os context <ID>` and `ai-os-paths ticket <ID>` all look in both places. What changes is
only whether the record sits on the small hot working surface (`tickets/*`, one level) or
in the historical one (`tickets/archive/*/*`, two levels) — a directory listing of
`tickets/` gets smaller; nothing else does.

Eligibility is `state` alone: `done` or `cancelled`, and not already archived. Nothing else
— not age, not id, not a recency window. `archive` with no `--apply` only previews the
plan; nothing moves until `--apply` is given, and a ticket already referenced by
in-flight follow-up work is exactly as archivable as a much older one, by design (the
resolver makes "still referenced" harmless: it is looked up by id either way).

Archived records are **read-only through this CLI**: `log` and `checkpoint` refuse them by
name rather than silently writing to a directory nobody browses any more. There is no
`unarchive` command — nothing in this workspace's actual workflow has needed one yet, and
one is not built ahead of that need; moving a directory back under `tickets/<ID>/` by hand
and re-running `ai-os tickets index --write` is the whole of what it would take to look at.

## Why this exists

A ticket's status was being written down in six places — its own `task.md`, the project
board's table, `context/current.md`, `context/checkpoint.md`, two sections of `state.md`
and the backlog line — and kept in agreement by hand. Hand-synchronised copies drift, and
a reader who opens the wrong one is told something false with full confidence.

So: **`tickets/<ID>/task.md` is the only place a ticket's status is declared.** Everything
else that shows status is generated from the records, between markers, and `doctor` fails
when a generated block no longer matches what the records say. A view that can go stale
silently is not allowed to exist.

## What doctor will and will not fail on

It reads *structure* — frontmatter fields, the directory name, the artifact manifest, the
generated block — never prose. A ticket id mentioned in a daily log, a session record or a
document is a mention, not a declaration, and is none of this command's business. The one
duplicate-authority rule is structural and exact: two records may not both declare
`id: <THE-SAME-ID>` in their frontmatter.

It reports; it changes nothing. `index --write` is the only writing path, it touches only
the region between the markers, and it refuses a file whose markers are missing.

Zero non-stdlib dependencies.
"""
